WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.468 --> 00:00:02.969
- What's happening now in the
United States is very similar

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:05.271
to what happened to Detroit
at the Industrial Revolution,

00:00:05.271 --> 00:00:07.941 align:start size:94% position:6%
right, which is that
you're seeing this change

00:00:07.941 --> 00:00:09.642
from industrial to digital.

00:00:09.642 --> 00:00:12.812
And Miami is the only city
in America that got the memo

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:16.016 align:start size:88% position:13%
and has basically built
its entire economy

00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:18.918 align:start size:97% position:3%
to capitalize on what I call
a tsunami of opportunity.

00:00:18.918 --> 00:00:21.554 align:start size:84% position:16%
And cities like New
York and San Francisco

00:00:21.554 --> 00:00:23.757
and Silicon Valley thought
they were too big to fail,

00:00:23.757 --> 00:00:25.825
and I'll tell you,
Dave, they're not.

00:00:25.825 --> 00:00:27.293 align:start size:84% position:16%
The world is more
disruptive than ever.

00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:29.729 align:start size:97% position:3%
Companies that are massive
get disrupted out of existence

00:00:29.729 --> 00:00:30.730 align:start size:75% position:25%
(snaps fingers)
like that.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:32.665 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yep.
- And we are building

00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:35.068
for the future, and
it's exciting to see.

00:00:35.068 --> 00:00:37.771
(downbeat music)

00:00:42.175 --> 00:00:43.443
- All right, I'm Dave Ruben.

00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:46.546
We are at the Local
Studio here in Miami.

00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:48.581 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yes, yes.
- And I am with the mayor

00:00:48.581 --> 00:00:51.985 align:start size:88% position:13%
of Miami, Francis
Suarez, who loves Miami.

00:00:51.985 --> 00:00:53.420
Fair to say you love Miami?

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:54.988
Nobody loves Miami.

00:00:54.988 --> 00:00:56.656
Everyone loves Miami, but
like you love, love Miami.

00:00:56.656 --> 00:00:58.792 align:start size:97% position:3%
- There's no separation
between me and Miami, right.

00:00:58.792 --> 00:01:00.560
Like, first of all,
just coming here

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:04.197 align:start size:97% position:3%
and seeing this incredible
studio in the heart of Wynwood

00:01:04.197 --> 00:01:05.965
in Miami, which is one
of the most dynamic,

00:01:05.965 --> 00:01:07.500
fun, exciting neighborhoods,

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.502 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's totally on
brand for you, right.

00:01:09.502 --> 00:01:12.739
And, you know, it's just
exciting to see you here,

00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:16.209 align:start size:97% position:3%
and we totally embrace what
you're doing, you know,

00:01:16.209 --> 00:01:17.844
in terms of the content,

00:01:17.844 --> 00:01:19.846 align:start size:84% position:16%
the conversations
that you're provoking,

00:01:19.846 --> 00:01:23.116
and it's very much in line
with who we are as a city.

00:01:23.116 --> 00:01:25.185
So it's just fun and
exciting to be here.

00:01:25.185 --> 00:01:27.020 align:start size:88% position:13%
- So I asked my guys
right before we started.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:32.025 align:start size:97% position:3%
I sat down with you in
Miami Beach in June of 2021.

00:01:33.493 --> 00:01:34.727
- That's right, almost a year.
- So it's about a little less

00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:36.162
than a year and a half ago.

00:01:36.162 --> 00:01:37.530
I was not a Floridian.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.332 align:start size:88% position:13%
I was not really
thinking about Florida.

00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:40.867
There was a little
bit of it, kind of.

00:01:40.867 --> 00:01:42.102 align:start size:81% position:19%
- A flicker, there
was a flicker.

00:01:42.102 --> 00:01:43.770
- There was a little something,

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:46.439
but a lot has changed
for me in this company.

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:48.875
And you're basically
in charge of the city

00:01:48.875 --> 00:01:52.545 align:start size:84% position:16%
that everyone is
coming to right now.

00:01:52.545 --> 00:01:53.813
How do you feel about that?

00:01:53.813 --> 00:01:55.348
Because I know it's good,

00:01:55.348 --> 00:01:57.951
but it also comes with some
stuff that's tough, right.

00:01:57.951 --> 00:02:00.353 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Sure, so look, first of all,
as the first mayor of Miami

00:02:00.353 --> 00:02:01.888
that was born in Miami, right,

00:02:01.888 --> 00:02:04.924 align:start size:88% position:13%
in the 125 year history
of Miami, this is
a dream come true.

00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:07.894
I feel like every day
I'm living a dream

00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:11.131
in so far as I was born
here, I grew up here,

00:02:11.131 --> 00:02:13.500
went to college
here, law school.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.034
- Your dad was mayor here.

00:02:15.034 --> 00:02:16.503
- My dad was a mayor from
when I was eight years old

00:02:16.503 --> 00:02:18.905 align:start size:94% position:6%
to when I was 16, so I
saw this sort of evolution

00:02:18.905 --> 00:02:21.441
of the city very up close.

00:02:21.441 --> 00:02:24.677
And to be in a situation
where we have set up a city

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:26.713
to be successful when
a lot of urban cities

00:02:26.713 --> 00:02:28.815 align:start size:84% position:16%
in America are not
successful or failing,

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:31.451 align:start size:97% position:3%
and we did it because we
followed three simple rules.

00:02:31.451 --> 00:02:33.920
We keep taxes low,
we keep people safe,

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.388
and we lean into innovation.

00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:38.158 align:start size:97% position:3%
So you know, we have created
a city that's number one

00:02:38.158 --> 00:02:39.225
in tech job growth, right.

00:02:39.225 --> 00:02:41.060
Number one in wage growth.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:43.596 align:start size:97% position:3%
We have a city that, because
we've kept taxes low,

00:02:43.596 --> 00:02:46.633 align:start position:0%
has grown 12%, which is the
second most in recorded history.

00:02:46.633 --> 00:02:48.568
And with all that
additional revenue,

00:02:48.568 --> 00:02:49.836
even though you lower taxes,

00:02:49.836 --> 00:02:51.437 align:start size:84% position:16%
you actually have
more revenue, right.

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:53.006
You know, spoiler alert for
New York and California.

00:02:53.006 --> 00:02:54.207
(Dave laughs)

00:02:54.207 --> 00:02:56.176 align:start size:88% position:13%
Right, we have actually
done the opposite

00:02:56.176 --> 00:02:57.377
of what other cities have done,

00:02:57.377 --> 00:02:58.878 align:start size:84% position:16%
which have defunded
their police.

00:02:58.878 --> 00:03:00.880 align:start position:0%
We've increased funding for
police, more officers than ever.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:02.849
Another shocking correlation.

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:04.784
Our crime is about to be,
God-willing, you know,

00:03:04.784 --> 00:03:06.319 align:start size:94% position:6%
I knock on wood.
- We're right there, yeah.

00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:09.022
- We're not in December yet,
but we are on the precipice

00:03:09.022 --> 00:03:11.724 align:start position:0%
of having the lowest per capita
homicide rate in history.

00:03:11.724 --> 00:03:12.926
- Yeah.

00:03:12.926 --> 00:03:14.627 align:start size:88% position:13%
- So, it's exciting time
to be here in Miami.

00:03:14.627 --> 00:03:16.496
So much has changed in
the last year and a half.

00:03:16.496 --> 00:03:19.232
- And it's everywhere in
Miami, not just right here,

00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:21.701 align:start size:97% position:3%
because although I don't
want to say the specific part

00:03:21.701 --> 00:03:23.002 align:start size:81% position:19%
of town that I
live in, you know,

00:03:23.002 --> 00:03:24.304
and in the area that I live in,

00:03:24.304 --> 00:03:26.472 align:start size:88% position:13%
there hasn't been a home
break in in 14 years.

00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:29.709
I mean the flourishing
is, every day I wake up

00:03:29.709 --> 00:03:32.045 align:start size:97% position:3%
and there's a new Los
Angelino coming to my town.

00:03:32.045 --> 00:03:33.479
Now, I'm starting to worry, but.

00:03:33.479 --> 00:03:35.014 align:start size:97% position:3%
- And I've been in an
elected office for 13 years,

00:03:35.014 --> 00:03:36.349
so maybe there's a correlation

00:03:36.349 --> 00:03:37.517 align:start size:94% position:6%
(Dave laughs)
between the 14 and the 13.

00:03:37.517 --> 00:03:40.220
But look, I think you're right.

00:03:41.521 --> 00:03:43.423 align:start size:88% position:13%
Miami was the best kept
secret, number one.

00:03:43.423 --> 00:03:45.024
Number two, Miami
has always punched

00:03:45.024 --> 00:03:46.259
above its weight, right.

00:03:46.259 --> 00:03:48.094
When you think about
what is Miami, I mean,

00:03:48.094 --> 00:03:50.496 align:start position:0%
you could arguably have
someone who lives in Palm Beach

00:03:50.496 --> 00:03:52.532
who is in Paris and they ask
them, "Where are you from?"

00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:53.800 align:start size:75% position:25%
They say Miami.
- Yeah.

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:56.469
- Like, just sort of
this big thing, right.

00:03:56.469 --> 00:03:57.837
I always say Miami's an idea.

00:03:57.837 --> 00:03:59.205
It's more than a city.

00:03:59.205 --> 00:04:01.841 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it's an idea
born out of a trauma.

00:04:01.841 --> 00:04:03.276
What's the trauma?

00:04:03.276 --> 00:04:05.011 align:start size:94% position:6%
The trauma is many people
in Miami were kicked

00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:07.747
out of their country
of birth, were exiled

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:09.949
because a leader in
their country said,

00:04:09.949 --> 00:04:11.618
"Give me all your businesses.

00:04:11.618 --> 00:04:12.819
"Give me all your property.

00:04:12.819 --> 00:04:14.687 align:start size:88% position:13%
"Don't worry, we'll
make everything equal."

00:04:14.687 --> 00:04:15.888
And you know what they did?

00:04:15.888 --> 00:04:17.056 align:start size:97% position:3%
They made everything equal.
- Yeah.

00:04:17.056 --> 00:04:18.458 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Equally miserable
for everybody,

00:04:18.458 --> 00:04:19.726 align:start size:72% position:28%
right.
(Dave laughs)

00:04:19.726 --> 00:04:21.127 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so we are
traumatized by that.

00:04:21.127 --> 00:04:22.795
And I think that's
why Miamians are

00:04:22.795 --> 00:04:25.198
so fundamentally pro-America.

00:04:25.198 --> 00:04:27.834 align:start size:84% position:16%
We're so fundamentally
pro, you know,

00:04:27.834 --> 00:04:29.902
sort of fair market
and free market.

00:04:29.902 --> 00:04:31.871 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we believe in freedom
and liberty, right.

00:04:31.871 --> 00:04:35.475 align:start size:97% position:3%
Those are principles that
for us, we fought for, right.

00:04:35.475 --> 00:04:38.411 align:start size:94% position:6%
We've been, my grandfather
was in jail in Cuba.

00:04:38.411 --> 00:04:40.079
His brother died in a
Cuban jail, you know.

00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:42.081
It's very, very real for us.

00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:44.317 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So on the tax side,
you wrote a piece

00:04:44.317 --> 00:04:45.818 align:start size:84% position:16%
in Wall Street
Journal about a month

00:04:45.818 --> 00:04:47.587 align:start size:94% position:6%
or month and a half ago
talking about free markets

00:04:47.587 --> 00:04:50.590
and capitalism and why
this place is thriving.

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:52.091
So how does it make any sense?

00:04:52.091 --> 00:04:53.760 align:start size:94% position:6%
I thought you need, the
government just needs more

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:55.061
of our money to
make things work,

00:04:55.061 --> 00:04:56.629
but you're keeping taxes low.

00:04:56.629 --> 00:04:57.964
And we don't have, you know,

00:04:57.964 --> 00:04:59.966
roads exploding and
all sorts of stuff.

00:04:59.966 --> 00:05:01.467
- Yeah, it's so
counterintuitive.

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:02.602
- How is it possible?

00:05:02.602 --> 00:05:03.836 align:start size:84% position:16%
How is it possible?
- So let's,

00:05:03.836 --> 00:05:05.204 align:start size:84% position:16%
let me give you a
real example, right.

00:05:05.204 --> 00:05:07.473 align:start size:88% position:13%
If you are living in
New York City right now,

00:05:07.473 --> 00:05:09.208 align:start size:97% position:3%
cost of living differential
between New York

00:05:09.208 --> 00:05:10.677
and Miami is two to one.

00:05:10.677 --> 00:05:12.945 align:start position:0%
So in New York, the median home
price is a million dollars.

00:05:12.945 --> 00:05:16.115
In Miami, median home price
is 540, two to one, right.

00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:18.785
In addition to that, in
New York, you're paying,

00:05:18.785 --> 00:05:20.386 align:start size:84% position:16%
if you're in the
highest tax bracket,

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:21.587
a lot of New Yorkers are,

00:05:21.587 --> 00:05:22.955 align:start size:84% position:16%
because again, your
cost of living.

00:05:22.955 --> 00:05:24.257
- Well they're all
coming down here now

00:05:24.257 --> 00:05:25.491
- Well, but check it out.

00:05:25.491 --> 00:05:26.993 align:start size:94% position:6%
Let's do the math, right.
- Yeah.

00:05:26.993 --> 00:05:29.429 align:start size:97% position:3%
- You're paying 54 cents out
of every dollar in taxes.

00:05:29.429 --> 00:05:31.097 align:start size:84% position:16%
That means that for
every dollar you earn,

00:05:31.097 --> 00:05:33.032
you take home 46 cents.

00:05:33.032 --> 00:05:35.802
In Miami, you get
63 cents, right.

00:05:35.802 --> 00:05:38.004
It is a huge delta, right.

00:05:38.004 --> 00:05:39.672
And so that, when you,

00:05:39.672 --> 00:05:44.677 align:start position:0%
at scale obviously is
incredibly motivating to people.

00:05:46.012 --> 00:05:47.647 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, I've seen statistics
that 70% of the taxes

00:05:47.647 --> 00:05:49.816 align:start size:84% position:16%
in New York are paid
by 8,000 people.

00:05:49.816 --> 00:05:52.952 align:start size:88% position:13%
And if that becomes
7,500 and 7,000, 6,500,

00:05:52.952 --> 00:05:55.421
you get what's called
a vicious cycle, right.

00:05:55.421 --> 00:05:57.156 align:start size:84% position:16%
You get massive
holes in your budget

00:05:57.156 --> 00:05:58.658
when those people leave.

00:05:58.658 --> 00:06:00.193
And there's only one way to
get more revenue for them,

00:06:00.193 --> 00:06:02.962
which is to increase taxes,
which creates more migration.

00:06:02.962 --> 00:06:03.896 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Yep.
- More holes,

00:06:03.896 --> 00:06:05.631
and I call it the death spiral.

00:06:05.631 --> 00:06:08.634
So what's happening now in the
United States is very similar

00:06:08.634 --> 00:06:11.070
to what happened to Detroit
at the Industrial Revolution,

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:13.706 align:start size:94% position:6%
right, which is that
you're seeing this change

00:06:13.706 --> 00:06:15.408
from industrial to digital,

00:06:15.408 --> 00:06:18.578
and Miami is the only city
in America that got the memo

00:06:18.578 --> 00:06:21.781 align:start size:88% position:13%
and has basically built
its entire economy

00:06:21.781 --> 00:06:24.617 align:start size:97% position:3%
to capitalize on what I
call tsunami of opportunity.

00:06:24.617 --> 00:06:27.320 align:start size:84% position:16%
And cities like New
York and San Francisco

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:29.522
and Silicon Valley thought
they were too big to fail.

00:06:29.522 --> 00:06:31.557
And I'll tell you,
Dave, they're not.

00:06:31.557 --> 00:06:33.025 align:start size:84% position:16%
The world is more
disruptive than ever.

00:06:33.025 --> 00:06:35.528 align:start size:97% position:3%
Companies that are massive
get disrupted out of existence

00:06:35.528 --> 00:06:36.362 align:start size:75% position:25%
(snaps fingers)
like that.

00:06:36.362 --> 00:06:37.530
- Yep.

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:39.165 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And we are building
for the future,

00:06:39.165 --> 00:06:40.666
and it's exciting to see.

00:06:40.666 --> 00:06:43.469 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Do you get any pushback from
other mayors who are going,

00:06:43.469 --> 00:06:46.506
"Hey Francis, you know,
if you keep taxes low,

00:06:46.506 --> 00:06:48.141
"it's gonna make us look
like a bunch of idiots,

00:06:48.141 --> 00:06:49.776
"because we got the
grift going here.

00:06:49.776 --> 00:06:52.578
"and it's kind of working until
it doesn't work, obviously."

00:06:52.578 --> 00:06:53.813
- You know, I'll tell you this,

00:06:53.813 --> 00:06:55.314
I'm president of the US
conference of Mayors,

00:06:55.314 --> 00:06:56.549 align:start size:94% position:6%
so I'm president of all
the mayors in the country.

00:06:56.549 --> 00:06:59.385
And what I get more of,
and I gotta be careful

00:06:59.385 --> 00:07:01.087
how I say this, is
I get more of the,

00:07:01.087 --> 00:07:02.321
"Camera's not on, listen,

00:07:02.321 --> 00:07:04.357
"we really think what
you're doing is great."

00:07:04.357 --> 00:07:05.658
- [Dave] Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:07:05.658 --> 00:07:07.126
- "But we don't have
the ability to do it."

00:07:07.126 --> 00:07:08.961
It's, that's more the sentiment
that I get, right, which is.

00:07:08.961 --> 00:07:10.630
- So why would a mayor or
not have the abilities?

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:11.898
Because of the local?

00:07:11.898 --> 00:07:13.332
- I think there's a
variety of reasons.

00:07:13.332 --> 00:07:14.534 align:start position:0%
I think they get a lot of
pressure from the press, right,

00:07:14.534 --> 00:07:16.869
which has this, their
own agenda, right,

00:07:16.869 --> 00:07:18.104
Which is a grow government,

00:07:18.104 --> 00:07:20.106
government is a solver
of every problem agenda.

00:07:20.106 --> 00:07:22.675
I think they've gotta
deal with institutions.

00:07:22.675 --> 00:07:25.311
Like for example, when I
first got elected in 2009,

00:07:25.311 --> 00:07:27.880 align:start size:97% position:3%
I was the only candidate
elected without union support

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:29.982
because I wouldn't lie to
the unions and tell them,

00:07:29.982 --> 00:07:31.417 align:start size:81% position:19%
"Look, we're gonna
raise your pay

00:07:31.417 --> 00:07:33.453 align:start size:97% position:3%
"when we're in a recession,
right, you know."

00:07:33.453 --> 00:07:36.956
And when we have to cut 20%
of our budget in one year,

00:07:36.956 --> 00:07:39.058 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is another thing
that's another lie, right,

00:07:39.058 --> 00:07:41.227
which is that government
can't balance its budget.

00:07:41.227 --> 00:07:44.330
Listen, I had to cut 20%
from my budget in one year,

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:47.166
$150 million out of a
$600 million budget.

00:07:47.166 --> 00:07:48.835
If I can cut 20% in one year,

00:07:48.835 --> 00:07:50.570 align:start size:94% position:6%
we can balance our budget
at the federal level.

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:52.171 align:start size:81% position:19%
- And the city
didn't fall apart.

00:07:52.171 --> 00:07:53.439
- And the city, no,
on the contrary,

00:07:53.439 --> 00:07:54.507
it started thriving, right.

00:07:54.507 --> 00:07:55.741
So we balanced our budget.

00:07:55.741 --> 00:07:57.543 align:start size:84% position:16%
We were at 13
million in reserves

00:07:57.543 --> 00:07:59.745
at the time, 2009 in November.

00:07:59.745 --> 00:08:01.380
Now we're at 150
million reserves,

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:02.882
the most reserves
we've ever had.

00:08:02.882 --> 00:08:06.219
We have the highest bond rating
under my administration ever

00:08:06.219 --> 00:08:08.020 align:start size:97% position:3%
in the history of the city,
which is double A plus,

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:09.989 align:start size:94% position:6%
one away from the highest,
which is triple A.

00:08:09.989 --> 00:08:12.258 align:start size:84% position:16%
So we're incredibly
fiscally sound.

00:08:12.258 --> 00:08:14.327
And it's all by just, you know,

00:08:14.327 --> 00:08:15.862
sort of following
the same formula

00:08:15.862 --> 00:08:17.363 align:start size:88% position:13%
that you would if you're
a homeowner, right.

00:08:17.363 --> 00:08:19.699
Like in your own personal,
save a little bit of money,

00:08:19.699 --> 00:08:21.033
pay off your credit
cards, you know,

00:08:21.033 --> 00:08:24.070 align:start size:84% position:16%
pay your debt, don't
overspend, right.

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:25.738 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then spend
strategically on the things

00:08:25.738 --> 00:08:27.673
that are gonna make
you more successful.

00:08:27.673 --> 00:08:29.509
Invest in yourself,
invest in, you know,

00:08:29.509 --> 00:08:31.577
policing, keeping people
safe, that kind of thing.

00:08:31.577 --> 00:08:34.180 align:start size:97% position:3%
- What kind of pushback do you
get from the Democrats here

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:35.414 align:start size:84% position:16%
when you're doing
some of these things?

00:08:35.414 --> 00:08:36.582 align:start size:94% position:6%
So you're lowering taxes.
- Yeah.

00:08:36.582 --> 00:08:38.618 align:start size:84% position:16%
- You're putting
police on the street.

00:08:38.618 --> 00:08:40.319
I mean, one thing for sure
that I notice here is that

00:08:40.319 --> 00:08:42.822
when I go to New York and I
see cops all over the place,

00:08:42.822 --> 00:08:45.224
to me, it's a sign that
something is not right.

00:08:45.224 --> 00:08:46.425 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Right.
- When I go here

00:08:46.425 --> 00:08:47.860 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I see cops, whether
it's in South Beach

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:50.062 align:start size:97% position:3%
or down where I am, it's
like, "Oh, they're patrolling

00:08:50.062 --> 00:08:51.430
"and just making sure
everything's okay."

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:53.299 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's a huge difference
in mentality.

00:08:53.299 --> 00:08:54.534
- Yeah, and I think
the other difference

00:08:54.534 --> 00:08:56.068
in mentality is we
support them, right.

00:08:56.068 --> 00:08:58.704
I think, in other cities,
they don't feel supported.

00:08:58.704 --> 00:09:00.206
- Yeah.

00:09:00.206 --> 00:09:01.707
- What you gotta understand
is, right now in America,

00:09:01.707 --> 00:09:03.643
being a police officer is
the hardest job in America.

00:09:03.643 --> 00:09:05.545 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's harder than
being a roofer, okay.

00:09:05.545 --> 00:09:07.146 align:start size:84% position:16%
You've got, there
isn't a job in America

00:09:07.146 --> 00:09:09.882
where you get paid less
money with more scrutiny

00:09:09.882 --> 00:09:11.951 align:start size:84% position:16%
and more risk, right,
and more at stake

00:09:11.951 --> 00:09:13.452
and then being asked
to do more things,

00:09:13.452 --> 00:09:14.620
because now they're being
asked to be social workers.

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:16.088
I mean, it's really, really hard

00:09:16.088 --> 00:09:17.723 align:start size:84% position:16%
to be a police
officer in America.

00:09:17.723 --> 00:09:19.926 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think
fundamentally, number one,

00:09:19.926 --> 00:09:21.861
they need to be protected.

00:09:21.861 --> 00:09:23.663
They need to be sort
of supported, right.

00:09:23.663 --> 00:09:26.866
Number two, they need
to be well paid, right.

00:09:26.866 --> 00:09:28.734
Like anybody in life, right,

00:09:28.734 --> 00:09:30.570 align:start size:84% position:16%
you've got to support
them financially

00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:32.338 align:start size:88% position:13%
because they've gotta
take care of their kids.

00:09:32.338 --> 00:09:34.240
They want their kids to go
to school just like you do.

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:35.841
And so we've been blessed.

00:09:35.841 --> 00:09:37.777
And then you have to have
a lot of police officers

00:09:37.777 --> 00:09:39.145
because it creates
a deterrent effect.

00:09:39.145 --> 00:09:42.048
We've given them a ton
of technology to be,

00:09:42.048 --> 00:09:44.216 align:start size:84% position:16%
to help them leverage
their, you know,

00:09:44.216 --> 00:09:45.451
their sort of force multiplier.

00:09:45.451 --> 00:09:47.219 align:start size:88% position:13%
And all of that has been
incredibly successful

00:09:47.219 --> 00:09:49.322 align:start size:94% position:6%
in maintaining our
homicide rate where it is.

00:09:49.322 --> 00:09:50.656
- What are some of the stresses

00:09:50.656 --> 00:09:52.391
of having all these
people move here?

00:09:52.391 --> 00:09:53.893
I mean, house prices for sure.

00:09:53.893 --> 00:09:55.494
You mentioned they're
lower than New York,

00:09:55.494 --> 00:09:57.830
but it's tough to
get a place here.

00:09:57.830 --> 00:09:59.532 align:start size:94% position:6%
And one of the things
I'm always worried about,

00:09:59.532 --> 00:10:02.068
and I see emails from
this, is that people

00:10:02.068 --> 00:10:03.603 align:start size:94% position:6%
that are the OG Floridians
- Sure.

00:10:03.603 --> 00:10:05.571
- Who have done it right and
lived here for a long time,

00:10:05.571 --> 00:10:07.873 align:start size:97% position:3%
they're suddenly feeling,
"Oh, we could get priced out

00:10:07.873 --> 00:10:11.377
"because all of these rich
people from LA and New York,

00:10:11.377 --> 00:10:12.878
"et cetera, are coming."

00:10:12.878 --> 00:10:14.280
- Yeah, look, it's, we are a
victim of our success, right.

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:15.982 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Yeah.
- What happens is,

00:10:15.982 --> 00:10:17.483 align:start size:84% position:16%
first of all, you have
historic inflation.

00:10:17.483 --> 00:10:19.185 align:start size:88% position:13%
So here's a problem
with government saying,

00:10:19.185 --> 00:10:21.621
"We're gonna solve
the inflation crisis

00:10:21.621 --> 00:10:23.322 align:start size:84% position:16%
"by giving everybody
money, right."

00:10:23.322 --> 00:10:25.825
So you're in a level where
we're at historic inflation.

00:10:25.825 --> 00:10:28.361
If you look at urban America
right now, not just Miami,

00:10:28.361 --> 00:10:31.797
urban America, rents
are up 25% or more

00:10:31.797 --> 00:10:34.367
in every urban city,
big city in America.

00:10:34.367 --> 00:10:36.836
Miami's more like 35, 45%.

00:10:36.836 --> 00:10:38.137
Why?

00:10:38.137 --> 00:10:39.705
Because you layer on top
of that a hyper demand.

00:10:39.705 --> 00:10:41.774
We're all, people are just
fleeing, getting the heck

00:10:41.774 --> 00:10:44.677
out of places where they
think they're not wanted

00:10:44.677 --> 00:10:47.079 align:start size:97% position:3%
or where they think they're
getting taxed to death

00:10:47.079 --> 00:10:49.315 align:start size:97% position:3%
or where they think the
economy is moving away from.

00:10:49.315 --> 00:10:50.549 align:start size:84% position:16%
So you have.
- I know the feeling.

00:10:50.549 --> 00:10:52.018 align:start size:88% position:13%
- You have all these
things happening, right.

00:10:52.018 --> 00:10:54.353 align:start position:0%
And so there, that is definitely
putting pressure on Miami.

00:10:54.353 --> 00:10:55.855
No doubt about it.

00:10:55.855 --> 00:10:58.524 align:start size:97% position:3%
I would be lying if I didn't
say that were the case.

00:10:58.524 --> 00:11:00.059
But what makes us different,

00:11:00.059 --> 00:11:02.528
and why will we thrive in
the face of that stress?

00:11:02.528 --> 00:11:03.896
Couple of things.

00:11:03.896 --> 00:11:06.165
One is New York is
completely built out.

00:11:06.165 --> 00:11:08.334 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think they built the last
building in Park Avenue

00:11:08.334 --> 00:11:10.336
in the last 50 years,
just got built, right.

00:11:10.336 --> 00:11:12.304 align:start size:94% position:6%
San Francisco's
artificially constrained,

00:11:12.304 --> 00:11:13.506
right, with their zoning code.

00:11:13.506 --> 00:11:16.208 align:start size:84% position:16%
Miami has the
ability to grow 10 X.

00:11:16.208 --> 00:11:17.677 align:start size:88% position:13%
We're in a
neighborhood right now,

00:11:17.677 --> 00:11:18.978
Wynwood, which has
explosive growth.

00:11:18.978 --> 00:11:20.446 align:start size:81% position:19%
You see it when
you walk out here.

00:11:20.446 --> 00:11:21.380 align:start size:88% position:13%
- You can feel it here.
- You can feel it.

00:11:21.380 --> 00:11:22.915
- It's ready to have, yeah.

00:11:22.915 --> 00:11:24.150
- We have the ability to grow
the delta between what's built

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:25.818 align:start size:88% position:13%
and what can be built
as a 10 to one multiple.

00:11:25.818 --> 00:11:29.755
So we can supply ourselves
at a level where, you know,

00:11:29.755 --> 00:11:31.624 align:start size:84% position:16%
where we can keep
prices relatively low.

00:11:31.624 --> 00:11:33.392 align:start size:84% position:16%
We have, just to give
you some numbers,

00:11:33.392 --> 00:11:36.295
about 185,000 dwelling
units in the city proper.

00:11:36.295 --> 00:11:38.097 align:start size:84% position:16%
In the pipeline
right now to be built

00:11:38.097 --> 00:11:41.767
in the next 24 to 36 months,
we have 47,000 dwelling units.

00:11:41.767 --> 00:11:44.770
So that's a 25% increase in
our entire building stock.

00:11:44.770 --> 00:11:46.072
So that's number one.

00:11:46.072 --> 00:11:48.374
Number two, we do
affordable housing

00:11:48.374 --> 00:11:50.810
in a way that is sort of
private sector oriented.

00:11:50.810 --> 00:11:52.611 align:start size:88% position:13%
We do the carrot
approach, not the stick.

00:11:52.611 --> 00:11:53.646
We don't tell tell developers,

00:11:53.646 --> 00:11:55.047
"This is what you have to do."

00:11:55.047 --> 00:11:56.615
We tell developers,

00:11:56.615 --> 00:11:57.883
"Look, we wanna be a part
of your capital stack.

00:11:57.883 --> 00:11:59.885
"We will do, you know,
we'll give you a dollar

00:11:59.885 --> 00:12:01.887 align:start size:88% position:13%
"for every $15 that
you invest in a project

00:12:01.887 --> 00:12:03.556 align:start size:84% position:16%
"or every $20 you
invest in a project,

00:12:03.556 --> 00:12:06.125
"and the only caveat for
our dollar is you gotta

00:12:06.125 --> 00:12:07.827
"maintain rent at
a certain level

00:12:07.827 --> 00:12:08.928
"for a certain period of time."

00:12:08.928 --> 00:12:10.463
That's it, it's a carrot.

00:12:10.463 --> 00:12:12.264
You want the carrot, this is
what you gotta do to get it.

00:12:12.264 --> 00:12:13.199
If you don't want it, it's okay.

00:12:13.199 --> 00:12:14.133
We're not gonna force you.

00:12:14.133 --> 00:12:15.468
- So they can build either way.

00:12:15.468 --> 00:12:17.169
- Absolutely, a hundred percent.

00:12:17.169 --> 00:12:18.437 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Oh, that's interesting.
- They can do market.

00:12:18.437 --> 00:12:19.939
Or if they wanna do
something different,

00:12:19.939 --> 00:12:21.207
they can do affordable housing.

00:12:21.207 --> 00:12:22.875
And we have built, and
we will build probably

00:12:22.875 --> 00:12:24.944
between four and a
half and $6 billion

00:12:24.944 --> 00:12:27.747 align:start size:97% position:3%
of affordable housing over
the next five to 10 years here

00:12:27.747 --> 00:12:29.348 align:start size:84% position:16%
in Miami, in addition
to the supply.

00:12:29.348 --> 00:12:31.517
- Can you also talk about
the homeless situation,

00:12:31.517 --> 00:12:34.754 align:start size:97% position:3%
because there basically
isn't a homeless situation,

00:12:34.754 --> 00:12:36.322
at least compared to
other major cities.

00:12:36.322 --> 00:12:37.857
So Florida, it's pretty nice.

00:12:37.857 --> 00:12:40.659
Seems like people would want
to come down here, right, so.

00:12:40.659 --> 00:12:42.394 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Don't give any ideas.
- Yeah, no, no.

00:12:42.394 --> 00:12:43.629
I mean, what have you done?

00:12:43.629 --> 00:12:44.630 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I'm sure a lot of
them watch your podcast.

00:12:44.630 --> 00:12:45.564 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah.
(Dave and Francis laugh)

00:12:45.564 --> 00:12:47.233
20% Of my audience is homeless.

00:12:47.233 --> 00:12:49.368
- So look, we're blessed.

00:12:49.368 --> 00:12:51.704
We have systematically invested,

00:12:51.704 --> 00:12:53.839
and we did it in a way that
was really smart, right.

00:12:53.839 --> 00:12:55.541 align:start size:94% position:6%
So we have something
called the homeless trust,

00:12:55.541 --> 00:12:57.176
and it is a tax, right.

00:12:57.176 --> 00:12:59.311
We don't like to pay taxes,
but this is a tourist tax.

00:12:59.311 --> 00:13:00.913 align:start size:84% position:16%
So this is for
people who are coming

00:13:00.913 --> 00:13:02.681
to Miami, staying in hotels.

00:13:02.681 --> 00:13:05.384 align:start size:81% position:19%
They pay 1% on
their bill, right,

00:13:05.384 --> 00:13:06.952
and it funds a homeless trust.

00:13:06.952 --> 00:13:08.654
So basically our visitors,

00:13:08.654 --> 00:13:10.122
not our residents,
are paying this tax.

00:13:10.122 --> 00:13:12.558 align:start size:94% position:6%
Our visitors are basically
paying a tax that goes

00:13:12.558 --> 00:13:14.827 align:start size:97% position:3%
to help what I call the
least, the last, and the lost,

00:13:14.827 --> 00:13:17.229 align:start size:84% position:16%
the most marginalized
in our community.

00:13:17.229 --> 00:13:19.465
And so, you know,
what have we done?

00:13:19.465 --> 00:13:21.600 align:start size:88% position:13%
We built a decentralized
set of centers

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:24.870 align:start size:94% position:6%
that give them alcohol
treatment, drug treatment,

00:13:24.870 --> 00:13:27.640
mental health treatment, and
vocational training, right.

00:13:27.640 --> 00:13:30.276
In addition to that,
we're spending over
the next two years,

00:13:30.276 --> 00:13:32.878
about $7 million in a plan
that I call functional Zero.

00:13:32.878 --> 00:13:34.914 align:start size:84% position:16%
So we have 640
homeless right now.

00:13:34.914 --> 00:13:36.715
6-4-0, according to our
last census, that's it.

00:13:36.715 --> 00:13:38.350 align:start size:88% position:13%
- That's it?
- That's it, that's it.

00:13:38.350 --> 00:13:40.186 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Wow.
- But I wanna be at zero

00:13:40.186 --> 00:13:42.288
because I don't think any
city in America has really,

00:13:42.288 --> 00:13:44.557 align:start position:0%
big city, has really challenged
themselves to get to zero.

00:13:44.557 --> 00:13:46.959
And I think that if
we create a model,

00:13:46.959 --> 00:13:49.662 align:start size:88% position:13%
then it can be certainly
exported, right,

00:13:49.662 --> 00:13:51.664 align:start size:88% position:13%
to the rest of the
country that are dealing

00:13:51.664 --> 00:13:54.366
with tens of thousands of
homeless in their cities.

00:13:54.366 --> 00:13:56.135
- Yeah, and again, this
is one of those things

00:13:56.135 --> 00:13:57.603
where I just see
these other mayors.

00:13:57.603 --> 00:13:58.804 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's like, "Why
don't you just look

00:13:58.804 --> 00:14:00.172
"at Miami and fix things?"

00:14:00.172 --> 00:14:01.607 align:start size:81% position:19%
I went to Denver.
- Yeah.

00:14:01.607 --> 00:14:03.409 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I did not, you know,
Denver is not San Francisco.

00:14:03.409 --> 00:14:04.643 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah.
- Denver is not LA.

00:14:04.643 --> 00:14:06.111
- [Francis] I love
the mayor of Denver.

00:14:06.111 --> 00:14:07.680 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, but I was like,
"But what's going on here?"

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:10.015 align:start position:0%
- Look, I think, I had a mayor,
and I won't say who it was,

00:14:10.015 --> 00:14:12.751 align:start size:97% position:3%
from a big city, one of the,
one of the top 20 cities

00:14:12.751 --> 00:14:15.054 align:start size:97% position:3%
in America, tell me recently,
"We just waited too long.

00:14:15.054 --> 00:14:16.555
"You know, we didn't
do what you guys did.

00:14:16.555 --> 00:14:18.657
"We used to have 9,000 homeless.

00:14:18.657 --> 00:14:21.293 align:start size:94% position:6%
"We brought it from 9,000
to a thousand, right.

00:14:21.293 --> 00:14:22.628
"And a thousand
is in the county,

00:14:22.628 --> 00:14:24.296
"which is 2.8 million people.

00:14:24.296 --> 00:14:26.265
"Only about 600 of
that thousand is

00:14:26.265 --> 00:14:29.101
"in the actual city proper,
which is my jurisdiction."

00:14:29.101 --> 00:14:32.538
So we did it through this
decentralized network.

00:14:32.538 --> 00:14:34.139
A lot of these cities
just didn't do it.

00:14:34.139 --> 00:14:36.275 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then of course, we're
seeing a lot of mismanagement,

00:14:36.275 --> 00:14:39.612 align:start size:97% position:3%
a lot of, I mean, I've
heard some crazy statistics

00:14:39.612 --> 00:14:42.314 align:start size:94% position:6%
in California where people
are paying like four

00:14:42.314 --> 00:14:45.184
or $500,000 a homeless
or something like that.

00:14:45.184 --> 00:14:46.018 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Oh yeah.
- And it's just money that's,

00:14:46.018 --> 00:14:47.486
there's no controls.

00:14:47.486 --> 00:14:50.155
It's rife with, you know,
a variety of problems.

00:14:50.155 --> 00:14:52.625
And so that obviously
is not gonna work.

00:14:52.625 --> 00:14:55.027 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Let's talk a little bit
about the hurricane situation.

00:14:55.027 --> 00:14:55.861 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Sure.
- Because obviously,

00:14:55.861 --> 00:14:57.029
it ended up a hurricane,

00:14:57.029 --> 00:14:58.297 align:start size:88% position:13%
and it ended up hitting
Southwest Florida.

00:14:58.297 --> 00:14:59.565
We're obviously in
southeast Florida,

00:14:59.565 --> 00:15:01.433 align:start size:84% position:16%
but at first it
was gonna hit here.

00:15:01.433 --> 00:15:02.601
- [Francis] Yeah, yep.

00:15:02.601 --> 00:15:04.169 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Obviously hurricanes
do hit Miami.

00:15:04.169 --> 00:15:05.704 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Yeah.
- You've been,

00:15:05.704 --> 00:15:08.173
you've been tweeting about it
and doing all sorts of stuff.

00:15:08.173 --> 00:15:11.110
What do we need to know for
the Miami and the future

00:15:11.110 --> 00:15:13.178 align:start size:97% position:3%
of Miami and who's gonna
come down here or to Florida

00:15:13.178 --> 00:15:15.481 align:start position:0%
in general when it comes to
these storms and all this stuff?

00:15:15.481 --> 00:15:17.216
- So to be honest, and
I'm, you know, again,

00:15:17.216 --> 00:15:19.118 align:start size:97% position:3%
as president of the US
Conference of Mayors, I was,

00:15:19.118 --> 00:15:21.987
I led a delegation
to the Gulf Coast

00:15:21.987 --> 00:15:23.989
to see all the cities
that were impacted

00:15:23.989 --> 00:15:26.525 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the Gulf Coast, and
it was devastating.

00:15:26.525 --> 00:15:30.129
And there's two things that I
think everybody needs to know.

00:15:30.129 --> 00:15:32.298 align:start size:84% position:16%
