that they don't know
what they're doing with,

00:31:39.197 --> 00:31:40.665
right, in their own home.

00:31:40.665 --> 00:31:42.267
So that's the, that's the
prototypical scenario.

00:31:42.267 --> 00:31:44.769
So it's not like we want
to take guns off the street

00:31:44.769 --> 00:31:47.071
in that sense, or we
want people not to.

00:31:47.071 --> 00:31:48.706
It's completely
voluntary, right.

00:31:48.706 --> 00:31:50.008
- Right.

00:31:50.008 --> 00:31:51.576
- So people wanna
do it, they do it.

00:31:51.576 --> 00:31:52.543
If they don't wanna do it,
they don't have to do it.

00:31:52.543 --> 00:31:54.012
- Do we even have a gun problem?

00:31:54.012 --> 00:31:54.712 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, is there, are there
shootings here really?

00:31:54.712 --> 00:31:55.980
Like, no.

00:31:55.980 --> 00:31:57.482 align:start size:88% position:13%
- So that's the other
thing is that, you know,

00:31:57.482 --> 00:32:00.018 align:start size:94% position:6%
in this conversation about
gun violence, people,

00:32:00.018 --> 00:32:02.086
it's such a myopic
conversation, right.

00:32:02.086 --> 00:32:05.823
We don't talk about the fact
that in Miami, for example,

00:32:05.823 --> 00:32:08.693
we have, we were ranked the
happiest city in America,

00:32:08.693 --> 00:32:12.163 align:start size:97% position:3%
the healthiest city in
America with 1.4% unemployment

00:32:12.163 --> 00:32:14.699
and increasing funding
for police departments.

00:32:14.699 --> 00:32:16.901 align:start size:94% position:6%
So it turns out if you
have more police officers,

00:32:16.901 --> 00:32:18.736
happy people, healthy people,

00:32:18.736 --> 00:32:20.571
and people that are
working, guess what?

00:32:20.571 --> 00:32:22.040
They're not killing
other people, right.

00:32:22.040 --> 00:32:24.475 align:start size:84% position:16%
So that's the
formula for success.

00:32:24.475 --> 00:32:26.611
- The happiness may come
from not being killed.

00:32:26.611 --> 00:32:27.879 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And by the way,
that has nothing to do

00:32:27.879 --> 00:32:29.614
with whether they own a
gun or don't own a gun.

00:32:29.614 --> 00:32:32.050
That has to do with
their frame of mind.

00:32:32.050 --> 00:32:33.885 align:start size:97% position:3%
It has to do with where
they, what they are in life.

00:32:33.885 --> 00:32:35.653 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I've often said,
"Look, you know,

00:32:35.653 --> 00:32:38.089 align:start size:88% position:13%
"if somebody wants to
hurt somebody, there are

00:32:38.089 --> 00:32:41.025
"unfortunately a multitude of
ways that that can happen."

00:32:41.025 --> 00:32:43.828
And so we've gotta look at the
root cause of why people feel

00:32:43.828 --> 00:32:46.464
compelled to do X and
where we're missing.

00:32:46.464 --> 00:32:49.100
And I think that gets lost
in the discussion a lot.

00:32:49.100 --> 00:32:50.768 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Do you think the
happy part is, I mean,

00:32:50.768 --> 00:32:52.503
and literally it's not,
you're not making it up.

00:32:52.503 --> 00:32:54.839
I mean, it was voted
the happiest city in
the United States.

00:32:54.839 --> 00:32:56.107
- Yeah, multiple
years by the way.

00:32:56.107 --> 00:32:57.608
- You can see, you
can see it here.

00:32:57.608 --> 00:32:58.776
I mean, people, and it's
not just the weather there.

00:32:58.776 --> 00:33:00.044 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think it obviously
is connected

00:33:00.044 --> 00:33:01.279 align:start size:88% position:13%
to the spirit of freedom
and all those things.

00:33:01.279 --> 00:33:02.480 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And taxes, when
you don't have to pay

00:33:02.480 --> 00:33:03.982 align:start size:84% position:16%
so much in taxes,
you're happier, right.

00:33:03.982 --> 00:33:07.218
Like, you know, at the end
of the day, we want to look,

00:33:07.218 --> 00:33:09.887 align:start size:94% position:6%
I have this concept called
return on time, right.

00:33:09.887 --> 00:33:11.556
We think about return
on investment, right.

00:33:11.556 --> 00:33:12.790 align:start size:81% position:19%
ROI, right, and
we think about it.

00:33:12.790 --> 00:33:14.158
And people don't
realize that their

00:33:14.158 --> 00:33:16.427
most precious commodity
is their time, right.

00:33:16.427 --> 00:33:17.595
It's the only thing
that we all have

00:33:17.595 --> 00:33:19.430
in common that is finite, right.

00:33:19.430 --> 00:33:20.932
We can't make more of it.

00:33:20.932 --> 00:33:22.567
I mean, we can be healthy
and hopefully, you know.

00:33:22.567 --> 00:33:24.569 align:start position:0%
We have good genetics, you
know, hopefully we live longer,

00:33:24.569 --> 00:33:28.206 align:start size:84% position:16%
but we're all
basically conforming

00:33:28.206 --> 00:33:30.842 align:start size:88% position:13%
to a life that is a
certain amount of time.

00:33:30.842 --> 00:33:33.378
And I think we have
gotta build our city

00:33:33.378 --> 00:33:35.079
around that concept.

00:33:35.079 --> 00:33:37.382
And if you see the world,
it's going to that concept.

00:33:37.382 --> 00:33:39.083
It's experiential, right?

00:33:39.083 --> 00:33:42.220
Restaurants, virtual
reality, metaverse.

00:33:42.220 --> 00:33:46.524 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's content, podcasts,
YouTube, et cetera.

00:33:46.524 --> 00:33:49.327
And then it's sports.

00:33:49.327 --> 00:33:52.897
It's, you know, experiential
and parks and things like that.

00:33:52.897 --> 00:33:55.733 align:start size:88% position:13%
And that's how you build
a city that I think

00:33:55.733 --> 00:33:59.003
really respects people's
most precious commodity.

00:33:59.003 --> 00:34:01.372
- On that note, we're gonna
take a ten second break.

